  {
   "q": "Identify the types of instruction formats in computer architecture. Explain each with examples.",
   "answer": [
    "1. Zero Address Instruction Format",
    "Used in stack-based architectures .",
    "No explicit operand field — operations are performed on the top of the stack .",
    "Example:",
    "PUSH 5",
    "PUSH 3",
    "ADD ; adds top two stack values → result = 8",
    "Use Case: Arithmetic in stack machines (e.g., JVM).",
    "🔹 2. One Address Instruction Format",
    "Contains one explicit operand and assumes an accumulator (ACC) as the second operand.",
    "Suitable for simple machines.",
    "Example:",
    "LOAD A ; ACC ← M[A]",
    "ADD B ; ACC ← ACC + M[B]",
    "STORE C ; M[C] ← ACC",
    "Use Case: Early computers (e.g., IBM 7090).",
    "🔹 3. Two Address Instruction Format",
    "Contains two explicit operands .",
    "Typically: OP Destination, Source.",
    "First operand acts as both input and output.",
    "Example:",
    "MOV R1, A ; R1 ← M[A]",
    "ADD R1, B ; R1 ← R1 + M[B]",
    "Use Case: Common in CISC architectures like x86.",
    "🔹 4. Three Address Instruction Format",
    "Contains three explicit operands : two sources and one destination.",
    "Requires more bits, but allows more flexible and faster execution.",
    "Example:",
    "ADD R1, R2, R3 ; R1 ← R2 + R3",
    "Use Case: RISC architectures (e.g., MIPS, ARM).",
    "🔹 5. Variable Length Instruction Format",
    "Instructions have different sizes depending on operation and addressing mode.",
    "Saves memory but complicates decoding.",
    "Example (x86 ISA):",
    "MOV AL, 34H ; 2 bytes",
    "MOV EAX, [1000H] ; 5 bytes",
    "Use Case: Intel x86 processors.",
    "🔹 6. Fixed Length Instruction Format",
    "All instructions have the same size (e.g., 32 bits).",
    "Easier to fetch and decode in pipeline execution.",
    "Example (MIPS ISA):",
    "R-format: ADD R1, R2, R3",
    "I-format: LW R1, 100(R2)",
    "J-format: J LABEL",
